      "First & Second Laws in Chemical Systems: Enthalpy H = U + PV; reversible entropy increment dS = \\frac{dq_{rev}}{T}; Clausius inequality \\oint \\frac{dq}{T} \\le 0; for isolated systems \\Delta S_{univ} = \\Delta S_{sys} + \\Delta S_{surr} \\ge 0.",
      "Gibbs Free Energy & Spontaneity: G = H - TS; at constant T and P, criterion for spontaneity is \\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S < 0; system is in equilibrium when \\Delta G = 0.",
      "Clapeyron & Clausius-Clapeyron Equations: Clapeyron equation \\frac{dP}{dT} = \\frac{\\Delta H}{T \\Delta V}; for liquid-vapor transitions assuming ideal vapor \\Delta V \\approx V_g = \\frac{RT}{P}, integrates to Clausius-Clapeyron form: \\ln\\left(\\frac{P_2}{P_1}\\right) = -\\frac{\\Delta H_{vap}}{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{T_2} - \\frac{1}{T_1}\\right).",
      "Chemical Potential (\\mu): Partial molar Gibbs free energy \\mu_i = \\left(\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial n_i}\\right)_{T, P, n_{j \\neq i}}; matter spontaneously diffuses from higher to lower chemical potential until chemical equilibrium (\\mu_i^\\alpha = \\mu_i^\\beta) is reached.",
      "Gibbs-Duhem Equation: \\sum n_i d\\mu_i = -S dT + V dP; at constant T and P, \\sum x_i d\\mu_i = 0, proving that chemical potentials of components in a mixture are interdependent.",
      "van 't Hoff Reaction Isotherm: Relates reaction Gibbs free energy to reaction quotient Q and equilibrium constant K: \\Delta G = \\Delta G^\\circ + RT\\ln Q; at equilibrium \\Delta G = 0 \\implies \\Delta G^\\circ = -RT\\ln K_p.",
      "van 't Hoff Reaction Isochore: Quantifies temperature dependence of equilibrium constant: \\frac{d\\ln K_p}{dT} = \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ}{RT^2}; integrated form \\ln\\left(\\frac{K_2}{K_1}\\right) = \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ}{R}\\left(\\frac{1}{T_1} - \\frac{1}{T_2}\\right); endothermic (\\Delta H > 0) reactions favor products at higher T.",
      "Gibbs Phase Rule: F = C - P + 2 where F is degrees of freedom, C is number of components, and P is number of phases in equilibrium; for condensed systems with constant pressure F' = C - P + 1.",
      "One-Component System (Water System): Triple point (T = 0.0098°C, P = 4.58 mmHg) where ice, liquid water, and water vapor coexist in equilibrium (P = 3, C = 1 \\implies F = 0, invariant point); negative slope of ice-water fusion curve due to \\Delta V_{fusion} < 0 (ice density < liquid water).",
      "Two-Component Eutectic System (Pb-Ag System): Solid-liquid equilibrium in metallurgy; eutectic point at 303°C and 2.6% Ag where liquid alloy solidifies at lowest constant melting temperature; metallurgical basis for Pattinson's process of desilverization of lead.",
